  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1148 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nSTORMS THIS AFTERNOON/EVENING: \n\nLittle change in the synoptic pattern is expected today with \nhigh pressure parked to our northeast leading southeast flow \naloft. However, a few mesoscale developments will prove pivotal \nto the forecast. Monsoonal moisture will continue to trickle in \nbeneath the high this afternoon. Weak shortwave energy is \nexpected engage the existing moisture pool and produce another \nround of showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and evening. \nYesterday's storms produced rainfall rates of 2-3 inches per \nhour in places, enough for numerous Flash Flood Warnings on \nsusceptible burn scars and flood- prone terrain. This gave us \nhigh enough confidence to issue a Flash Flood Warning today for \nportions of southeast Utah and western Colorado with additional \ndynamic forcing from the shortwaves. Please see the Hydrology \nSection below for more information.\n\nPlease continue to monitor the forecast and adjust your plans \nas needed. Storms can develop rapidly, bringing sudden hazards.\n\nMIDWEEK OUTLOOK:\n\nBy midweek, the high-pressure system will shift slightly to the\neast, allowing a weak low pressure system to set up over the \nFour Corners region. While storms have recently been taking an \nunusual east-to-west path, the new pattern will likely steer \nthem up from the south and perhaps the west by late week. This \nwill also increase cloud cover across the region which will \nlower our confidence in storm strength, but increase the \ncoverage of showers and storms across the region. We will be \nmonitoring this aspect of the forecast closely. If there is \nenough clearing tonight, a Flash Flood Watch may be warranted \nagain tomorrow.\n\nDaytime highs will cool a handful of degrees tomorrow and \nreturn to seasonable or slightly below normal through Friday. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD: \n\nHigh pressure builds back in by the end of the week bringing \nwarmer temperatures with southwest flow aloft. The amount of \nmoisture available varies greatly between models by this point \nin the forecast, so forecast confidence decreases. However, \nthere is no real push of dry air across the region, so we \nanticipate enough moisture for afternoon showers and storms \nfavoring the mountains each day through early next week. The \nstrength, coverage, and hazards associated with this convection \nremain uncertain at this time.",
